```matlab
% Input values for parameters p, q, r
p = 5;
q = 3;
r = 2;

% Solve the system of equations for 'a', 'b', and 'c'
syms a b c p q r x
eq1 = ap + bq + cr == 28;
eq2 = a + b + c == 10;
eq3 = x == a + 2*b - c/3;
sol = solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], [a, b, c]);

% Extract the values of 'x' and substitute the solved values of 'a', 'b', and 'c'
x_val = subs(subs(x, sol), {a, b, c}, {sol.a, sol.b, sol.c});

% Display the result
disp(x_val)
```

This implementation assumes that `p`, `q`, and `r` are known input values. You can replace them with your own values as needed. The output of this script will display the calculated value of `x`. Note that `subs` is used twice here to replace both `x` and `a`, `b`, and `c` with their corresponding solved values. If you only need to replace one variable, you can omit the second argument to `subs`.

Yes, you can achieve this by implementing a custom validation rule using the JIRA ScriptRunner for Bitbucket Server plugin. Here's how:

1. Install the JIRA ScriptRunner for Bitbucket Server plugin.
2. In your JIRA instance, go to the cog icon (Settings) > Add-ons > Manage add-ons > Find new add-ons > Search for "ScriptRunner" > Install the JIRA ScriptRunner for Bitbucket Server plugin.
3. Restart your JIRA instance.
4. Create a new script by going to System > Script Console > New Script.
5. Paste the following Groovy code into the editor:

```groovy
import com.onemonks.scriptapi.validation.ValidationResult
import com.onemonks.scriptapi.validation.ValidationRule

class ChecklistCompleteValidationRule implements ValidationRule {
    @Field('checklistName')
    String checklistName = ''

    def validate(context) {
        def issue = context.issue
        def checklists = issue.getCheckItems()?.findAll { it.getSystem()?.name == 'checklist' }
        def completeChecklists = checklists.find { it.checkItemOrder.every { ci -> ci.isDone } }

        if (!completeChecklists && checklistName && checklists.any { it.name == checklistName }) {
            return new ValidationResult("Please complete '$checklistName' before moving this issue.")
        }

        return null
    }
}
```

6. Save and run the script to test it.
7. Go to the workflow configuration of the transition you want to apply the rule to.
8. Click on the validation rules tab and click the "Add Rule" button.
9. Select "Custom Script" and enter the name of your script class ("ChecklistCompleteValidationRule").
10. Enter the name of the checklist you want to check ("checklistName") into the script parameters field.
11. Save the validation rule.

Now, when an issue tries to move to the next column without completing the specified checklist, it will display an error message preventing the transition until the checklist is completed.

