Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function swapAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Find the index of the first adjective (excluding articles like "a" and "an")
  let firstAdjIndex = -1;
  for (let I = 0; I < words.length; i++) {
    const word = words[i].trim();
    if (word.match(/^[a-z]+\b/g)) {
      const isAdj = word.match(/e$/) || word.match(/ly$/) || word.match(/er$/) || word.match(/est$/);
      if (!isAdj && firstAdjIndex === -1) {
        firstAdjIndex = i;
      }
    }
  }

  // Swap the positions of the first and second adjectives
  if (firstAdjIndex !== -1 && firstAdjIndex > 0) {
    [words[firstAdjIndex], words[firstAdjIndex - 1]] = [words[firstAdjIndex - 1], words[firstAdjIndex]];
  }

  // Join the words back into a sentence
  return words.join(" ");
}

// Example usage
const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park.";
const swappedSentence = swapAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(swappedSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park."
```

This function works by splitting the input sentence into an array of words, finding the index of the first non-article adjective, and then swapping its position with the position of the previous word (which may also be an adjective). The function returns the modified array as a new string. Note that this implementation assumes that adjectives always come before the noun they modify, which may not be strictly true in all cases. If you need more sophisticated handling of adjectives, you may need to modify this function or use a different approach.

