To implement bubble sort in C++ with minimal memory usage and error prevention, we can follow these steps:

1. Define a function `bubbleSort` that takes the array and its size as arguments and performs the sorting algorithm.

2. Inside the function, we need two nested loops to iterate over the array elements. The outer loop keeps track of the number of passes (iterations) made through the array, and the inner loop swaps adjacent elements if they are in the wrong order.

3. To minimize memory usage, we don't need to create any additional arrays or structures. We just modify the original array in place.

4. To prevent potential errors, we can add bounds checking to ensure that the indices being accessed are within the bounds of the array. This will help avoid segmentation faults and other runtime errors.

Here's the implementation:

```cpp
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    bool swapped;
    for (int i = 0; i < n-1; ++i) {
        swapped = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < n-i-1; ++j) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        if (!swapped) break;
    }
}

