Here's the C code snippet to convert `argv` (command-line arguments) to a null-terminated C string:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

char *join_args(int argc, char **argv) {
    int i, len = 0;
    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        len += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add space between arguments
    }
    char *result = malloc(len);
    int pos = 0;
    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        strcpy(result + pos, argv[i]);
        pos += strlen(argv[i]);
        if (i != argc - 1) { // Don't add space before last argument
            result[pos++] = ' ';
        }
    }
    result[pos] = '\0';
    return result;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    char *joined = join_args(argc, argv);
    printf("Joined args: %s\n", joined);
    free(joined);
    return 0;
}
```

The `join_args()` function takes the `argc` and `argv` parameters as input, creates a new null-terminated string `result`, and then iteratively adds each argument to it while preserving the spaces in between. Finally, the function returns the resulting string. You can use this function in your `main()` function or anywhere else where you want to join command-line arguments into a single string. Note that we're using dynamic memory allocation here by calling `malloc()`. You may want to replace this with a fixed-size buffer or adjust the size calculation based on your specific requirements.

